⚡ Autonomous Email Triage Engine
An enterprise-grade, Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) automation system built in Make.com. Engineered to eliminate inbox distractions, this system processes incoming communications in a single daily batch, leveraging large language models (Groq / Llama 3.3) to classify intent, draft responses, and deliver a zero-distraction 9:00 AM digest to Slack.

🚀 The Problem It Solves
Constant inbox notifications destroy deep work, but ignoring communications risks missing critical opportunities. Fully autonomous AI email replies also carry a massive brand-safety risk.

This architecture solves both issues by acting as a smart executive assistant. Instead of continuous micro-interruptions, the system wakes up once a day, processes the last 24 hours of emails, and presents a clean daily report. AI drafts the perfect reply; a human clicks one button to approve and send it.

🛠️ Tech Stack & Integrations
Orchestration: Make.com (Optimized for Free-Tier constraints)

AI / LLM: Groq API (Llama-3.3-70b-versatile)

Communications: Slack API (Interactive Block Kit), Gmail API

Data Handling: JSON Parsing, Custom Webhooks, Regex/String Splitting, Data Aggregation

🧠 System Architecture & Modular Breakdown
To maximize Make.com's operational limits and fit entirely within the free-tier 2-scenario limit, this system utilizes advanced bundling, aggregation, and asynchronous webhooks.

Scenario 1: The 9 AM Evaluator & Bundler
This scenario wakes up once a day to ingest, process, and route the previous 24 hours of data.

Gmail (Watch Emails): Trigger scheduled to run strictly at 09:00, fetching all emails received since the last execution.

Groq (Create a Chat Completion): Each email body is passed to the LLM with a strict system prompt to output a JSON object containing:

urgency (High/Low)

category (Sales, Support, General)

summary (1-sentence overview)

draft_reply (A context-aware response)

JSON (Parse JSON): Strips markdown syntax from the Groq output and converts it into mappable data pills.

Router & Aggregation:

Route A (High Urgency / Sales Leads): Generates individual interactive Slack Alert Cards embedded with the AI's drafted response and an "Approve & Send" button.

Route B (Low Urgency / Support): Routes into a Text Aggregator module, which catches all low-priority AI summaries and bundles them into a single, clean "Daily Digest" list sent to Slack in one unified message.

💡 The Payload Handoff Trick:
Slack has strict character limits for interactive button values. To bridge the gap between Scenario 1 and Scenario 2, the "Approve & Send" button in Slack uses a piped format to pack multiple data points into a single string. It also utilizes escapeJSON() to ensure line breaks in the AI reply don't break the Slack Block Kit UI.

Payload format: "value": "{{threadId}}|{{escapeJSON(draft_reply)}}"

Scenario 2: The Executor
This scenario is entirely asynchronous and lies dormant until a human interacts with the Slack alert.

Webhooks (Custom Webhook): Listens for the incoming POST request triggered when a user clicks the "Approve & Send" button in Slack.

Tools (Set Multiple Variables): Uses Make's split() function to crack open the button's payload value using the pipe | delimiter.

Item 1 = The Gmail Thread ID

Item 2 = The finalized AI Draft Text

Gmail (Reply to an Email): Injects the extracted Draft Text directly into the extracted Thread ID, ensuring the email stays perfectly threaded with the original sender.

HTTP (Make a Request): Sends a callback to the Slack API to dynamically update the original message block, replacing the "Approve" button with a green "✅ Sent (Approved)" badge so other team members know the item is handled.

⚙️ Security, Constraints & Best Practices
Free-Tier Optimized: By relying on an Aggregator and a strict 24-hour batch schedule, this architecture processes high volumes of data while remaining well under Make.com's strict free-tier operation and scenario limits.

Zero AI Hallucination Risk: Because the webhook isolates the send trigger, an LLM hallucination can never accidentally email a client without human sign-off.

Escaped Variables: The LLM output is wrapped in escapeJSON() before being injected into a Slack HTTP request to prevent unescaped newlines from destroying the POST body payload.




